vealing \n       a private and intimate text, between the lines. The reader\n       has to actually feel her way around the primary text, to \n       get the full picture. Rationale gives way to inner \n       dialogue. The calm, reasoned voice of, \" I f I'm honest, I\n       think I was put off by my rather disarming tendency to \n       blush. At the slightest thing. I t always perplexed me,\" \n       covers subconscious embarrassment: \"Never do this again. \n       Avoid this at all cost. Confusion and distress cloak \n       themselves in shades of nonchalance. It's only a colour. \n       I'm fine. Really.\" The interaction of the materials \n       informs the process of reading and the unraveling \n       narrative.\" -- Vamp and Tramp website, accessed 4/25/2012 \n500    Condition: no library markings, in protective enclosure. \n500    Library copy is number 36 in an open edition, signed by \n       the artist. \n600 10 Weston, Heather. \n650  0 Blushing in art. \n650  0 Red in art. \n650  7 Binding method|vHardcover (hand-sewn)|2bkart \n650  7 Binding method|vCase binding (handmade)|2bkart \n650  7 Printing / production method|vUnknown.|2bkart \n655  7 Artists' books.|2lcgft \n655  7 Artists' books|zEngland.|2aat \n907    .b44798593 \n915    |9Baseline 9_2013|9Under Authority Control \n935    BJN-5183 \nFull Record\nOnline\nLocation \tCall # \tStatus \tNotes\nArt Library Artists' Book Collection \tN7433.4.W48 R42 2000 \tIn-Library Use Only \tNo. 36\n\nSubjects\nWeston, Heather.\nBlushing in art.\nRed in art.\nBinding method -- Hardcover (hand-sewn)\nBinding method -- Case binding (handmade)\nPrinting / production method -- Unknown.\nArtists' books.\nArtists' books -- England.\n\nDescription\nLanguage: \tEnglish\nNotes: \t\"This highly unusual artist's book uses heat-sensitive ink, that relies on the reader's inevitable contact with the page, to explore the ordinary and uncomfortable phenomenon of blushing. At first sight, the book contains a text that is reversed (white) out of red ink—like letters incised into a surface. But as the reader handles the book, the warmth from her fingers causes the ink to fade, revealing a private and intimate text, between the lines. The reader has to actually feel her way around the primary text, to get the full picture. Rationale gives way to inner dialogue. The calm, reasoned voice of, \" I f I'm honest, I think I was put off by my rather disarming tendency to blush. At the slightest thing. I t always perplexed me,\" covers subconscious embarrassment: \"Never do this again. Avoid this at all cost. Confusion and distress cloak themselves in shades of nonchalance. It's only a colour. I'm fine. Really.\" The interaction of the materials informs the process of reading and the unraveling narrative.\" -- Vamp and Tramp website, accessed 4/25/2012\nCondition: no library markings, in protective enclosure.\nCover title.\nLibrary copy is number 36 in an open edition, signed by the artist.\nText in white printed on red paper using a heat-sensitive ink that fades when touched to reveal additional hidden text.\nTitle on the red cloth cover is embossed.\nOCLC Number: \t51013173\nISBN: \t02072818474\nPersistent URL: \thttp://search.lib.unc.edu/search?R=UNCb4479859\n\tView this record in the Classic Catalog\n\nCatalog Home   |   Libraries & Collections   |   E-Research Tools   |   E-Journal Finder   |   Libraries Home\nWorldcat   |   Privacy Policy   |   Feedback  \n"}